WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.166 --> 00:00:01.793
I mean I got a mind of my own

00:00:01.835 --> 00:00:04.671
If you wanna put everybody
on the spot, I gotta put
you on the spot.

00:00:05.005 --> 00:00:06.548
What—what?

00:00:06.965 --> 00:00:07.966
It's only fair.

00:00:07.966 --> 00:00:09.050
See, that’s really not fair.

00:00:17.350 --> 00:00:22.981
I'm headed to Portland, Oregon, to
interview the Portland Trail Blazers
star Damian Lillard.

00:00:23.148 --> 00:00:27.152
So we're going out west to
find out what he thinks of himself.

00:00:28.903 --> 00:00:30.363
See you in six hours.

00:00:30.655 --> 00:00:33.616
Going over my notes about Dame
one more time before we land.

00:00:36.369 --> 00:00:39.789
Made it to Portland,
and it is raining of course.

00:00:45.503 --> 00:00:48.214
I met up with Dame
at the Blazers practice facility.

00:00:52.135 --> 00:00:53.136
 You gotta hold it up
 to this camera.

00:00:54.888 --> 00:00:55.889
 Like a pro.

00:00:56.347 --> 00:00:57.057
 Cut!

00:00:57.057 --> 00:00:58.016
 [Laughing]

00:01:03.897 --> 00:01:07.525
 What is it like playing
 in the Western Conference?

00:01:07.776 --> 00:01:10.320
 It's a war zone.
 Every game is tough.

00:01:10.320 --> 00:01:12.530
 Who’s the most fatal opponent
 in that war zone?

00:01:13.198 --> 00:01:14.365
 I think Golden State.

00:01:15.366 --> 00:01:18.161
 Cuz you can beat Golden State
 for three-and-a-half quarters.

00:01:19.204 --> 00:01:24.834
 It could be six minutes left,
 you up 15, and they will erase
 that lead in two minutes.

00:01:25.335 --> 00:01:31.341
 I think where people get it
 mixed up is when they allow that to
 turn into a fear factor.

00:01:31.591 --> 00:01:38.640
 I think sometimes people fear them,
 and when they sense that little
 bit of fear, you done.

00:01:38.848 --> 00:01:42.102
 I mean, you gotta go out
 there and play against them
 like you know you gonna beat ‘em.

00:01:42.268 --> 00:01:43.561
 Give ‘em something
 to think about.

00:01:43.937 --> 00:01:47.857
 How did it feel to be swept
 in the opening round of
 the playoffs in 2018?

00:01:48.108 --> 00:01:53.530
 That was the one that
 really stung because not only
 were our expectations so high

00:01:53.530 --> 00:01:59.702
 to make a push, but we
 lost in the first round, and we got
 swept with home-court advantage.

00:01:59.702 --> 00:02:03.706
 So it was like, after we lost
 to New Orleans, I was just hurt for,
 like, a month.

00:02:03.706 --> 00:02:06.709
 I was just—I didn’t wanna watch
 basketball, nothing.

00:02:06.709 --> 00:02:07.836
 I just was hurt.

00:02:09.129 --> 00:02:12.799
 OK, so every guest I have—
 I ask you to take me to a place

00:02:12.799 --> 00:02:14.843
 That is what we're going
 to do right now

00:02:14.968 --> 00:02:15.635
 You ready?

00:02:15.635 --> 00:02:16.219
 OK.

00:02:16.386 --> 00:02:18.054
 Take me to your first tattoo.

00:02:18.138 --> 00:02:22.559
 My mom was dropping me off at school. It was the day before my first day of college.

00:02:22.976 --> 00:02:25.854
 We went to the mall, and there was a tattoo shop in there.

00:02:25.854 --> 00:02:28.189
 We walked in, and I got my first tattoo.

00:02:28.940 --> 00:02:31.359
 And it was like the Warner Brothers sign.

00:02:31.401 --> 00:02:35.113
 But it was an “LB” instead of a “WB,” and I got “Lillard Brothers” above it.

00:02:35.113 --> 00:02:39.659
 So my brother got the same tattoo, and that was my first tattoo.

00:02:39.659 --> 00:02:42.370
 Take me to the first music album you bought.

00:02:42.579 --> 00:02:47.625
 The first album I bought was "Tha Block is Hot", Lil Wayne.

00:02:48.042 --> 00:02:52.714
 [Singing] Tha block is hot, tha block is hot, ha ha, tha block is hot.

00:02:52.797 --> 00:02:56.843
 Take me to the time that you realized college scouts were wrong about you.

00:02:56.843 --> 00:03:00.263
 I went to Adidas Nations in Chicago after my sophomore year.

00:03:01.514 --> 00:03:02.473
 And I had a real good camp.

00:03:03.224 --> 00:03:07.353
 But then, like, after the camp was over, they did, like, all the evaluations, and they was like,

00:03:07.353 --> 00:03:11.608
 “Yeah, you know, not really a good jump-shooter off the dribble.”

00:03:11.608 --> 00:03:15.820
 -It was like—it was stuff that I really disagreed with.
 -Course, you’re, like, known for now.

00:03:15.820 --> 00:03:19.032
 Like, I didn't take it personal, but I was like—I really disagreed with it.

00:03:19.032 --> 00:03:21.951
 I was like, “No, I don’t think—I don't agree with that.”

00:03:21.951 --> 00:03:22.202
 Yeah.

00:03:22.202 --> 00:03:25.914
 And that was the first time I was like, “They wrong.”

00:03:26.497 --> 00:03:28.416
 Like, they wrong. Like, they hatin’.

00:03:28.416 --> 00:03:29.125
 That was like—

00:03:29.375 --> 00:03:30.251
 “They hatin’ on me!”

00:03:30.251 --> 00:03:32.045
 It was typical, but I was like, “They hatin’.”

00:03:32.045 --> 00:03:33.755
 I never told nobody I felt that way.

00:03:37.842 --> 00:03:41.638
 Social media went kinda crazy after a quote came out

00:03:41.638 --> 00:03:45.350
 where you were talking about not wanting to sell yourself out for titles.

00:03:54.150 --> 00:03:56.819
 I don't feel I need to prove to anybody how bad I wanna win it

00:03:56.819 --> 00:04:00.406
 cuz it shows in my work. It shows in my approach to the game.

00:04:00.406 --> 00:04:03.868
 I'm not gonna go and try to team up with top players in the league

00:04:04.285 --> 00:04:07.080
 cuz I know that’ll make the chances better because that's not who I am.

00:04:07.330 --> 00:04:10.083
 You know, I’m a “get it out the mud” type of person.

00:04:10.500 --> 00:04:14.545
 It’s been a good time to have to work and grind and do all this stuff

00:04:14.545 --> 00:04:19.217
 for everything that I get cuz when I get it, I'm like, “I worked for this. I earned it.”

00:04:19.217 --> 00:04:21.469
 And it’s more gratifying, you know what I'm sayin’?

00:04:21.469 --> 00:04:24.764
 So on the outside, they gonna say, “Oh, he'll never win in Portland.

00:04:24.764 --> 00:04:26.766
 They don't have enough. They don't have this.”

00:04:26.766 --> 00:04:31.437
 But to me, it's like, if we never get it done, we never get it done.

00:04:35.024 --> 00:04:39.487
 So is it accurate to say that failure for you isn’t not getting a ring;

00:04:39.487 --> 00:04:41.531
 failure for you is not being who you are?

00:04:41.864 --> 00:04:42.532
 Exactly.

00:04:43.074 --> 00:04:47.578
 Some of the best players ever—I was watching Michael Rapaport and he was talkin’ about,

00:04:47.578 --> 00:04:53.001
 “When you think about Gary Payton, you never think about him in Miami winning a ring.

00:04:53.001 --> 00:04:54.877
 You think about Gary Payton in Seattle.”

00:04:55.336 --> 00:04:57.714
 And Allen Iverson in Philly.

00:04:57.714 --> 00:05:00.550
 Like, you just think about their legacy and what they did.

00:05:00.883 --> 00:05:04.012
 For me, like I said, I just focus on what means something to me.

00:05:04.429 --> 00:05:07.348
 Working for it, and if it don't happen, I can live with it knowing that,

00:05:07.348 --> 00:05:10.226
 you know, the effort and the care was there.

00:05:10.226 --> 00:05:11.227
 You know, I went after it.

00:05:12.020 --> 00:05:18.151
 So this way that you live, it seems as though you have a code that you live by

00:05:18.151 --> 00:05:19.110
 Basically.

00:05:19.110 --> 00:05:20.570
 What is that code?

00:05:21.946 --> 00:05:25.575
 I mean, I think it's pretty simple, man. Just stay true.

00:05:25.658 --> 00:05:30.788
 You know, if you famous, you a professional athlete, celebrity,

00:05:30.788 --> 00:05:35.835
 whatever you wanna call it, and it's easy to do things that you might not do.

00:05:35.835 --> 00:05:39.339
 I'm not willing to do the other stuff and step outside of

00:05:39.339 --> 00:05:43.259
 being true to myself cuz that’s just not, just not who I am.

00:05:45.219 --> 00:05:50.850
 But this want to be loyal and stay true to who you are,

00:05:50.850 --> 00:05:53.936
 to me, seems very unique, especially for an NBA player.

00:05:53.936 --> 00:05:59.650
 I mean, I got a mind of my own, and I’m kinda just comfortable in my skin.

00:05:59.650 --> 00:06:01.027
 I'm comfortable with who I am,

00:06:01.027 --> 00:06:05.573
 so I know that I'm so much more than a basketball player, for one.

00:06:05.573 --> 00:06:09.369
 So for somebody to have criticisms or comments or

00:06:09.369 --> 00:06:12.747
 whatever about me as a basketball player, that’s—that’s perfectly fine.

00:06:17.168 --> 00:06:22.215
 If it came down to it, would you want to be remembered as being a loyal person,

00:06:22.215 --> 00:06:24.509
 or would you want to be remembered as being a champion?

00:06:24.509 --> 00:06:26.344
 Well, I think we all wanna be a champion

00:06:26.469 --> 00:06:31.849
 An NBA champion? You know, that's different than just being a champion in life.

00:06:31.849 --> 00:06:35.645
 When I'm done playin’ and when I walk away, nobody could ever say nothin’ bad about me

00:06:35.645 --> 00:06:39.482
 other than, “He didn't win a ring, but he was the realest dude that I worked with.

00:06:39.482 --> 00:06:42.527
 Even when he was a max player, he was always solid with me.”

00:06:42.735 --> 00:06:46.531
 And they'll respect me and appreciate me past my career for that.

00:06:46.739 --> 00:06:52.078
 How much of this code that you live by is based off the way you grew up in Oakland?

00:06:52.078 --> 00:06:54.705
 I think that’s naturally an attitude of people from Oakland.

00:06:54.705 --> 00:06:58.584
 It’s just like, “I'mma appreciate the battle and the hard times.

00:06:58.584 --> 00:07:02.213
 So when I overcome all of it, it’ll feel better.”

00:07:02.213 --> 00:07:05.925
 But I think it’s just the foundation of my family.

00:07:05.925 --> 00:07:07.552
 My AAU team, I played for the Oakland Rebels.

00:07:08.052 --> 00:07:13.599
 And the Rebels, we had to do fundraising just so we can afford plane tickets

00:07:13.599 --> 00:07:18.646
 and team shoes and to go play in these tournaments in Vegas and L.A. and all that stuff.

00:07:18.646 --> 00:07:23.151
 And it's another team across town, the Oakland Soldiers, they get all the best players.

00:07:23.151 --> 00:07:27.029
 And when I got to high school, I became one of the best players in the Bay Area,

00:07:27.029 --> 00:07:28.781
 and they wanted me to come play on their team.

00:07:29.282 --> 00:07:30.283
 Whatchu think I said?

00:07:31.200 --> 00:07:32.493
 I think you said no.

00:07:32.493 --> 00:07:33.453
 “No.”

00:07:33.453 --> 00:07:39.167
 I stayed with the same AAU program that used to pick me up from the BART station

00:07:39.167 --> 00:07:43.129
 and give me rides home, and when I didn’t have no money, pay for my food.

00:07:43.129 --> 00:07:46.757
 Like, I was loyal to the people and the environment that I was in.

00:07:47.341 --> 00:07:50.595
 What was the best part about growing up in Oakland?

00:07:50.595 --> 00:07:53.639
 I went to college in Utah, and when I got there,

00:07:54.140 --> 00:07:57.727
 I was just ready for it because I had been around so much.

00:07:57.727 --> 00:08:05.776
 I had been around gangs and gun violence and drugs and numbers and money.

00:08:05.776 --> 00:08:09.947
 Everybody in college is more kinda naive, and I was like,

00:08:09.947 --> 00:08:13.576
 “Man, like, Oakland really is sped up.”

00:08:13.576 --> 00:08:19.790
 It’s fast out there, so you—you know that growing up in Oakland, you can survive anywhere.

00:08:19.790 --> 00:08:21.959
 I got robbed at gunpoint in the 12th grade.

00:08:21.959 --> 00:08:26.339
 But it is tough when you grow up, and there's people out there that be in the streets.

00:08:26.422 --> 00:08:35.348
 The reason that I was able to be successful and kinda deal with that environment
 and still move on and have good grades in high school and go to college

00:08:35.348 --> 00:08:38.809
 and stuff like that is because of the kind of family I come from.

00:08:39.644 --> 00:08:44.273
 I would come home from school, and I got 10 cousins at my grandmother's house.

00:08:44.273 --> 00:08:50.196
 We all do our homework. We at the park, we outside together, and then at some point,
 somebody get in trouble: “Why you got a C-minus?”

00:08:50.404 --> 00:08:54.492
 If I misuse a word, my Aunt Van is like, “What did that word mean?

00:08:55.117 --> 00:08:57.328
 Use it in a sentence, past and present.”

00:08:57.537 --> 00:09:06.504
 I had that type of family that, like, challenged me on so many different
 levels that it was like I really had no choice but to be some type of successful.

00:09:06.504 --> 00:09:15.012
 And I just think it’s not fair that a lot of the kids, majority
 of the kids in my neighborhood, just didn't have that.

00:09:20.268 --> 00:09:22.687
 What kind of parent do you want to be?

00:09:23.062 --> 00:09:32.655
 I wanna be a great parent, but I wanna be the kind of parent where everything
 that I'm suggesting or teaching my son, I can also represent that.

00:09:32.905 --> 00:09:37.660
 I don't wanna have to deal with him challenging me on stuff that
 I've taught him and then I'm not doing it myself.

00:09:37.660 --> 00:09:44.542
 So I wanna just—I wanna be a great example,
 especially with him being an African-American man.

00:09:45.209 --> 00:09:46.335
 [Rapping] Alumni to slums

00:09:46.335 --> 00:09:47.837
 I’m polished, ain’t no removin’ that

00:09:47.837 --> 00:09:50.840
 Straight up outta Oakland, where knowledge and all the shooters at

00:09:50.840 --> 00:09:51.841
 I'm hoopin’ and producin’

00:09:51.841 --> 00:09:53.634
 Now they sayin’ that dude can rap

00:09:53.634 --> 00:09:55.636
 I seen it all right before my eyes

00:09:55.636 --> 00:09:56.596
 Now salute a mac

00:09:57.346 --> 00:09:58.097
 Four.

00:09:58.097 --> 00:10:02.393
 How much joy do you get from making music and being an artist?

00:10:04.604 --> 00:10:06.689
 I get so much joy outta making music.

00:10:07.106 --> 00:10:12.695
 I think because through the music, I’m able to
 express myself, and I can do it in a creative way.

00:10:12.695 --> 00:10:15.990
 Music gives people, like, a vibe, gives ‘em a feeling

00:10:15.990 --> 00:10:21.037
 When I listen to music and I'm hearing people say the lyrics, I take myself to what they ??

00:10:21.245 --> 00:10:23.873
 Now you're genuinely a good rapper.

00:10:23.873 --> 00:10:24.832
 You really feel that way?

00:10:25.041 --> 00:10:28.836
 100 percent I feel that way. You know I would tell you if I didn't think you were a good rapper.

00:10:28.836 --> 00:10:29.587
 Appreciate that.

00:10:29.587 --> 00:10:38.596
 Most NBA players—I wouldn’t say most.
 A lot of NBA players that try to rap aren't great rappers.

00:10:38.596 --> 00:10:39.555
 Would you agree with that?

00:10:39.930 --> 00:10:41.432
 I would agree with that.

00:10:41.432 --> 00:10:44.018
 OK. What other NBA players do you feel—
 -There’s some good ones.

00:10:44.310 --> 00:10:48.522
 Yeah. Do you feel like they can also genuinely rap?

00:10:48.522 --> 00:10:50.107
 I think Iman Shumpert can rap.

00:10:50.107 --> 00:10:51.067
 Mhmm.

00:10:51.067 --> 00:10:52.568
 I think Lonzo Ball can rap.

00:10:53.069 --> 00:10:54.070
 Really?

00:10:54.570 --> 00:10:56.155
 I mean, it's not my type of rap.

00:10:56.155 --> 00:10:59.533
 I rap like the music that I listen to, like Nas and Common.

00:10:59.700 --> 00:11:04.413
 So I rap like that, storytelling and, like, basically telling my reality.

00:11:04.413 --> 00:11:08.668
 When they lose people is when they come out and
 they start rapping about them playing basketball.

00:11:08.668 --> 00:11:11.754
 That's the fastest way to get people to be like, “This dude corny.”

00:11:11.754 --> 00:11:12.254
 Yeah.

00:11:12.254 --> 00:11:18.344
 You know what I'm sayin’? When they start talking about
 how they dropped 30 on somebody or did like—don't talk about it.

00:11:18.344 --> 00:11:19.303
 Just leave that alone.

00:11:19.679 --> 00:11:23.140
 Why do people think that you can't rap and play basketball? Why is that?

00:11:23.599 --> 00:11:24.892
 I don’t know. It’s a good question.

00:11:25.393 --> 00:11:27.812
 We got a lot more time on our hands than people think.

00:11:27.812 --> 00:11:28.771
 Yeah.

00:11:28.771 --> 00:11:31.273
 I can't let you leave without asking you this.

00:11:32.024 --> 00:11:38.030
 Every Friday, you have rappers send in freestyles for #4BarFriday.

00:11:38.030 --> 00:11:39.031
 Yeah.

00:11:39.365 --> 00:11:41.659
 Yeah, I’mma see how this one flow, you know what I mean?

00:11:41.659 --> 00:11:42.284
 It’s been a grip.

00:11:42.284 --> 00:11:43.577
 But I pull up like I’m parkin’

00:11:43.828 --> 00:11:45.121
 I'm Tony Stark with the talkin’

00:11:45.121 --> 00:11:45.955
 Yeah.

00:11:45.955 --> 00:11:47.748
 So that’s Mark Twain in a Mark X

00:11:48.040 --> 00:11:51.043
 Doin’ Mach 5, goin’ over your head like a pop fly

00:11:51.043 --> 00:11:51.836
 That’s four.

00:11:51.836 --> 00:11:54.463
 If you wanna put everybody on the spot, I gotta put you on the spot.

00:11:54.463 --> 00:11:58.008
 You gotta give me four bars about this interview.

00:11:58.008 --> 00:11:59.927
 What—what?

00:11:59.927 --> 00:12:00.928
 It's only fair.

00:12:01.637 --> 00:12:02.972
 I told Taylor, “Take me there.”

00:12:02.972 --> 00:12:04.140
 She say, “Where?”

00:12:04.140 --> 00:12:05.307
 I say, “A special place.”

00:12:05.307 --> 00:12:06.892
 I dream about equality.

00:12:06.892 --> 00:12:07.852
 A nightmare we segregate

00:12:08.352 --> 00:12:09.353
 Represent the people

00:12:09.353 --> 00:12:10.938
 Pave a way for us to elevate

00:12:11.230 --> 00:12:13.899
 Love one and educate instead of stress and medicate

00:12:14.108 --> 00:12:16.694
 Four bars, much love.

00:12:17.361 --> 00:12:18.362
 You ready?

00:12:19.780 --> 00:12:20.781
 Wrappin’ up with Dame Lillard.

00:12:20.781 --> 00:12:21.615
 What’s good?

00:12:21.615 --> 00:12:22.450
 How’d it feel?

00:12:22.450 --> 00:12:23.451
 It felt great.

00:12:23.451 --> 00:12:24.452
 Yes.

00:12:24.452 --> 00:12:24.994
 Blazers’ very own.

00:12:24.994 --> 00:12:25.745
 Wink, wink.

00:12:25.745 --> 00:12:26.746
 [Laughing] Wink, wink.

00:12:26.746 --> 00:12:28.164
 I can’t wink, either

